1
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:12,420
Now let's get into ip netns.

2
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:17,380
This is the Linux utility to do

3
00:00:17,380 --> 00:00:24,550
manipulation or work with
the network namespaces.

4
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:28,060
Just before we start,

5
00:00:28,060 --> 00:00:33,440
we'll talk about a process
relationship to namespaces.

6
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:41,470
Processes in general
inherit from a parent.

7
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:44,115
Even without namespaces,

8
00:00:44,115 --> 00:00:51,615
you have some process and it
can have children processes.

9
00:00:51,615 --> 00:00:54,105
That's normal.

10
00:00:54,105 --> 00:01:00,050
Then this is also the case
when we're using namespaces.

11
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:02,365
But within the namespace,

12
00:01:02,365 --> 00:01:06,180
the process ID has

13
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:10,420
the naming that's
associated with its parent,

14
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:14,060
but then also what's
in parentheses.

15
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:17,655
That's what's
visible internally.

16
00:01:17,655 --> 00:01:22,185
What's the main
takeaway from this

17
00:01:22,185 --> 00:01:28,180
is I want to introduce the
concept of a root namespace.

18
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:32,900
Whenever you're outside
of a container,

19
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:35,400
outside of a namespace
that you're executing,

20
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:38,060
you're in the root namespace.

21
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:41,260
Anything that you create is

22
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:46,450
a child of that and then
it would get some name.

23
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:52,495
As I mentioned, ip netns is

24
00:01:52,495 --> 00:01:58,300
the process network namespace
management utility.

25
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:03,720
It's a pretty simple to use
tool and we're going to

26
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:06,160
need to use it in
combination with

27
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:09,600
some other things to actually
make all of this work.

28
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,620
You can go to the man page,

29
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:12,740
but the commands we'll

30
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:15,510
need are going to
be pretty simple.

31
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:19,560
When you actually
think about it,

32
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:23,260
it seems like it
might be complicated,

33
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:26,700
but it's really broken down.

34
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:28,420
You break it down
into three parts.

35
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:31,445
There's only three things
that you need to do.

36
00:02:31,445 --> 00:02:34,870
You need to create the
network namespace,

37
00:02:34,870 --> 00:02:38,105
you then need to
create a veth pair,

38
00:02:38,105 --> 00:02:42,575
and then you need to attach
the veth to a namespace.

39
00:02:42,575 --> 00:02:44,870
We'll go through each of these.

40
00:02:44,870 --> 00:02:46,680
Then once you have that, you can

41
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:49,365
execute commands that
we're familiar with

42
00:02:49,365 --> 00:02:52,260
inside of the namespaces

43
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:55,230
or even outside of
the namespaces.

44
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:57,160
We can use Linux networking

45
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:00,080
to connect these all together.

46
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:04,680
Let's start with create
a network namespace.

47
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:11,330
But before I do that, let
me flip over to a terminal.

48
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:16,385
There's a GitHub repo
associated with this module.

49
00:03:16,385 --> 00:03:19,025
Here is the contents of it.

50
00:03:19,025 --> 00:03:22,880
What's provided is
a new vagrant file.

51
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:26,980
This vagrant file is
an example of one

52
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:32,370
that consists of two
different nodes.

53
00:03:32,370 --> 00:03:35,800
I'm actually able to use this

54
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:42,040
to create two virtual
machines and run them.

55
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:46,655
This is up here.

56
00:03:46,655 --> 00:03:50,240
Right here we're
defining the two nodes.

57
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:57,580
If I had specific provisioning

58
00:03:57,580 --> 00:03:59,340
just for this virtual machine,

59
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:03,765
I can put it inside of this
little piece right here.

60
00:04:03,765 --> 00:04:08,440
Let me delete that extra tab.

61
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:11,490
We can have that,

62
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:15,660
but then there's also
a full provisioning.

63
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:17,720
We install net tools

64
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:21,020
and T shark and docker
and things like that.

65
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:24,940
This would apply to
both virtual machines.

66
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:27,700
I could specify, again,

67
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,840
each of them gets one CPU,

68
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:33,100
one gig of memory, and so forth.

69
00:04:33,100 --> 00:04:35,050
I can say vagrant

70
00:04:35,050 --> 00:04:38,990
up and it will launch
both virtual machines

71
00:04:38,990 --> 00:04:41,750
or I could say vagrant up node

72
00:04:41,750 --> 00:04:45,140
1 and it will bring up node 1.

73
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:46,790
Or I could say vagrant up node

74
00:04:46,790 --> 00:04:49,195
2 and it'll just
bring up node 2.

75
00:04:49,195 --> 00:04:53,310
I brought up both

76
00:04:53,310 --> 00:04:57,010
of them and so that's
what I'm running here.

77
00:04:57,010 --> 00:04:59,830
If I look at what I did,

78
00:04:59,830 --> 00:05:02,110
what I didn't show is

79
00:05:02,110 --> 00:05:04,730
this one extra line we
didn't have before.

80
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:09,090
It's saying create a private
network and attach it to

81
00:05:09,090 --> 00:05:11,350
this virtual machine
and then create

82
00:05:11,350 --> 00:05:13,150
a virtual private network

83
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:15,220
and attach it to this
virtual machine.

84
00:05:15,220 --> 00:05:17,460
You'll see that the
IP addresses are on

85
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:19,470
the same subnet and they'll

86
00:05:19,470 --> 00:05:22,305
be able to communicate
with one another.

87
00:05:22,305 --> 00:05:27,100
We should be able to see
this If I say IP addr.

88
00:05:27,890 --> 00:05:31,305
This was the standard one

89
00:05:31,305 --> 00:05:34,410
that even if we didn't
specify that network,

90
00:05:34,410 --> 00:05:36,990
this allows me to SSH

91
00:05:36,990 --> 00:05:39,570
into the machine and
things like that.

92
00:05:39,570 --> 00:05:43,470
This is the one that's
newly created and you

93
00:05:43,470 --> 00:05:47,025
could see it has an IP
address associated with it.

94
00:05:47,025 --> 00:05:50,410
192.168.1.100.

95
00:05:50,690 --> 00:05:56,475
If I say paying 192.168.1.101,

96
00:05:56,475 --> 00:05:58,635
we'll see that
that's able to work.

97
00:05:58,635 --> 00:05:59,910
What that's doing is it's

98
00:05:59,910 --> 00:06:03,370
pinging the other
virtual machine.

99
00:06:04,340 --> 00:06:07,680
That's just before
we get started,

100
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:09,510
I wanted to point out

101
00:06:09,510 --> 00:06:13,440
that this vagrant file and
what we're actually running.

102
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:16,515
For this particular lesson,

103
00:06:16,515 --> 00:06:18,540
we're only going to use node1.

104
00:06:18,540 --> 00:06:21,310
But for a future one,

105
00:06:21,950 --> 00:06:25,755
both machines can be used,

106
00:06:25,755 --> 00:06:28,300
We'll be networking
between them.

107
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:33,570
Getting back to how to
recall the three steps.

108
00:06:33,570 --> 00:06:35,145
Create a network namespace.

109
00:06:35,145 --> 00:06:36,780
Create the V eight pair,

110
00:06:36,780 --> 00:06:40,245
and then attach the V eight
devices to a namespace.

111
00:06:40,245 --> 00:06:44,025
Here, to create a
network namespace,

112
00:06:44,025 --> 00:06:46,080
all you're doing is using

113
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:47,880
an IP netns command and

114
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:50,190
saying ad and then
giving it a name.

115
00:06:50,190 --> 00:06:51,750
As an example of this,

116
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:55,365
we could say ip
netns add nsDemo.

117
00:06:55,365 --> 00:06:56,940
That creates

118
00:06:56,940 --> 00:07:00,420
a new network namespace
and then I can list it.

119
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:08,625
Let me go over to the
terminal ip netns add nsDemo.

120
00:07:08,625 --> 00:07:15,060
I need to run it as
super user, so sudo.

121
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:21,640
Great. Now let's see this.

122
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:28,450
Great, I listed it and
we could see nsDemo.

123
00:07:29,350 --> 00:07:36,395
Then the next step is create
Virtual Ethernet devices.

124
00:07:36,395 --> 00:07:39,550
Veth is how they're abbreviated.

125
00:07:39,550 --> 00:07:45,585
Veth devices are always created
in interconnected pairs.

126
00:07:45,585 --> 00:07:47,265
You can see it as a pair.

127
00:07:47,265 --> 00:07:49,260
You always create them together.

128
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:52,560
Packets transmitted
on one device

129
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:56,100
in the pair immediately
shows up on the other.

130
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:59,130
It's basically just
creating a wire

131
00:07:59,130 --> 00:08:03,970
with network devices
attached to it.

132
00:08:04,850 --> 00:08:08,055
For us to create a veth pair,

133
00:08:08,055 --> 00:08:11,115
we use the IP link command,

134
00:08:11,115 --> 00:08:13,980
the IP link, which allows us

135
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:16,335
to create a variety
of other devices.

136
00:08:16,335 --> 00:08:19,455
This is going to allow us
to create a veth pair.

137
00:08:19,455 --> 00:08:24,420
We say IP link ad first name,

138
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:26,250
we're going to say it's type the

139
00:08:26,250 --> 00:08:28,050
veth and then we're going to say

140
00:08:28,050 --> 00:08:31,995
the peer name of it is
whatever the second name is.

141
00:08:31,995 --> 00:08:36,555
As the example ip
link add all of that,

142
00:08:36,555 --> 00:08:39,945
let's go type that in.

143
00:08:39,945 --> 00:08:43,395
Sudo ip. Well,

144
00:08:43,395 --> 00:08:45,465
first let's actually look at

145
00:08:45,465 --> 00:08:49,695
what devices there
are. Ignore docker0.

146
00:08:49,695 --> 00:08:53,745
We'll discuss what that
is in a future lesson.

147
00:08:53,745 --> 00:08:57,105
But these are the
physical devices.

148
00:08:57,105 --> 00:08:59,760
Now let's create the veth pair,

149
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:03,270
sudo ip link add

150
00:09:03,270 --> 00:09:07,390
the first part was
that first name vethY.

151
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:13,050
The second part what
type it is, type veth.

152
00:09:13,050 --> 00:09:18,015
Then we say what the
peer name is, vethZ.

153
00:09:18,015 --> 00:09:22,800
This should create two
devices, vethY and vethZ.

154
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:26,530
If I say IP link,

155
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:32,830
there they are, IP Y and Z.

156
00:09:33,020 --> 00:09:39,970
You can note that they're
both in the down state.

157
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:46,665
What we want to
do at some point,

158
00:09:46,665 --> 00:09:52,290
we could show it now
is sudo ip link set

159
00:09:52,290 --> 00:10:00,960
device vethY up
and then vethZ up.

160
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:02,910
Look at that again and we could

161
00:10:02,910 --> 00:10:05,920
see they're now in the state up.

162
00:10:07,430 --> 00:10:11,580
Now we've created our veth pair,

163
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:13,980
they're actually
devices, they're

164
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:17,175
just hanging out, doing nothing.

165
00:10:17,175 --> 00:10:20,640
There's nothing really to
do with them just yet.

166
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:23,940
The last part is going to

167
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:28,305
be creating or attaching
them to a namespace.

168
00:10:28,305 --> 00:10:30,450
But as a little bit of an aside,

169
00:10:30,450 --> 00:10:33,740
sometimes you get a little

170
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:38,630
lost and you don't know
what the peer actually is.

171
00:10:39,450 --> 00:10:43,405
There's a tool that you can run,

172
00:10:43,405 --> 00:10:47,260
veth tool where it

173
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:54,795
basically allows you to
discover the index of the peer.

174
00:10:54,795 --> 00:10:57,270
Let's take a quick
look at that.If I

175
00:10:57,270 --> 00:11:03,375
say ethtool -s vethY,

176
00:11:03,375 --> 00:11:05,880
one of the things that it lists.

177
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:08,940
This is a general
debugging tool that

178
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:10,420
you can see how many packets are

179
00:11:10,420 --> 00:11:12,790
going through this
and things like that.

180
00:11:12,790 --> 00:11:15,670
You can see peer index 5.

181
00:11:15,670 --> 00:11:21,260
Now if I say IP link,

182
00:11:23,490 --> 00:11:30,415
you can see this first
number is that index.

183
00:11:30,415 --> 00:11:35,935
If we look down for index 5,

184
00:11:35,935 --> 00:11:39,940
we can see that it's VEG.

185
00:11:39,940 --> 00:11:41,860
We ran ethtool,

186
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:48,220
vethY, it showed peer index 5.

187
00:11:48,220 --> 00:11:52,450
I come down into the IP link

188
00:11:52,450 --> 00:11:57,220
and let me say a grep for
the beginning of line 5,

189
00:11:57,220 --> 00:12:00,080
and it's going to
list out that one.

190
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:05,830
You might think, all
right, this is simple

191
00:12:05,830 --> 00:12:10,435
because it's actually
showing it right here.

192
00:12:10,435 --> 00:12:13,900
We'll see where that's
not the case in a minute.

193
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:18,400
But keep that in mind as a
tool that you can you use.

194
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:21,220
As I mentioned, the third step,

195
00:12:21,220 --> 00:12:23,125
we created the namespace.

196
00:12:23,125 --> 00:12:24,700
We created the veth pair.

197
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:29,995
Now we want to attach the
veth.device to a namespace.

198
00:12:29,995 --> 00:12:33,325
Here, we just say IP link set.

199
00:12:33,325 --> 00:12:40,690
Give it one of the name of
the device, the veth device.

200
00:12:40,690 --> 00:12:45,310
Then we say netns and then
the name of the namespace.

201
00:12:45,310 --> 00:12:49,075
In our example, we're going
to say IP link set vethZ,

202
00:12:49,075 --> 00:12:53,690
netsZ, netns, nsDemo.

203
00:12:55,110 --> 00:13:01,015
Let me clear this. I
want to say IP link

204
00:13:01,015 --> 00:13:07,820
set vethZ, netns, nsDemo.

205
00:13:08,430 --> 00:13:11,275
Forgot the pseudo again.

206
00:13:11,275 --> 00:13:15,460
There we go. Let me
look at IP link.

207
00:13:15,460 --> 00:13:19,870
You'll note that index 5,

208
00:13:19,870 --> 00:13:22,270
which was vethZ, actually

209
00:13:22,270 --> 00:13:26,420
disappeared and now
we only see vethY.

210
00:13:27,410 --> 00:13:32,715
What that means is it's no
longer in the root namespace.

211
00:13:32,715 --> 00:13:37,090
It's inside of the
namespace nsDemo.

212
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:48,070
Great. Now, what can
we do with this?

213
00:13:48,070 --> 00:13:50,755
Well, before we get into
what we can do with it,

214
00:13:50,755 --> 00:13:55,930
you can also attach to
a second namespace.

215
00:13:55,930 --> 00:13:58,990
We have this picture
shown up where here in

216
00:13:58,990 --> 00:14:02,395
the root namespace we have
vethY and we have vethZ.

217
00:14:02,395 --> 00:14:07,930
I could also create
a second namespace

218
00:14:07,930 --> 00:14:12,940
and then attach vethY into
the second namespace.

219
00:14:12,940 --> 00:14:16,120
What that will allow is nsDemo1

220
00:14:16,120 --> 00:14:22,615
to communicate with nsDemo2.

221
00:14:22,615 --> 00:14:25,315
Here's the commands that
you would run for that,

222
00:14:25,315 --> 00:14:27,415
create the two namespaces,

223
00:14:27,415 --> 00:14:29,005
create the veth pair,

224
00:14:29,005 --> 00:14:31,420
and then you're just
attaching vethZ

225
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:37,610
to nsDemo1 and vethY to nsDemo2.

226
00:14:38,310 --> 00:14:40,600
We're not going to show that,

227
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:44,840
but that's definitely
something that you can do.

228
00:14:45,420 --> 00:14:48,670
After we've set these up,

229
00:14:48,670 --> 00:14:53,590
we can now execute commands
inside of the namespace.

230
00:14:53,590 --> 00:14:56,900
We might want to say,

231
00:14:57,480 --> 00:14:59,950
I want to see that device,

232
00:14:59,950 --> 00:15:02,335
that veth device inside
of that namespace.

233
00:15:02,335 --> 00:15:06,400
I can run IP netns exec,

234
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:09,700
give it the name
of the namespace

235
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:12,730
and then the command
that we want to run.

236
00:15:12,730 --> 00:15:16,135
I might want to give
it an IP address.

237
00:15:16,135 --> 00:15:19,030
I run all of that IP addr

238
00:15:19,030 --> 00:15:21,760
add and I assign
it to that device.

239
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:25,315
I might want to set the
device to the upstate.

240
00:15:25,315 --> 00:15:33,020
All of those we can do
inside of the namespace.

241
00:15:33,810 --> 00:15:39,800
Let's go over and
try some of that.

242
00:15:42,630 --> 00:15:46,360
If we say, let me
clear all of that,

243
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:49,525
IP netns exec,

244
00:15:49,525 --> 00:15:52,465
nsDemo, that's the
name of our namespace.

245
00:15:52,465 --> 00:15:53,755
I want to look inside.

246
00:15:53,755 --> 00:15:57,980
I want to run the command
IP link inside of it.

247
00:15:59,790 --> 00:16:02,170
There we go, we could see

248
00:16:02,170 --> 00:16:05,875
vethZ and know it has
that index of five.

249
00:16:05,875 --> 00:16:09,145
This is where, using
that eth tool,

250
00:16:09,145 --> 00:16:11,950
if I need to find what
it's connected to,

251
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:13,855
this is one mechanism.

252
00:16:13,855 --> 00:16:16,360
I can look inside of the
different containers to try

253
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:21,110
to find that index.

254
00:16:21,330 --> 00:16:27,730
We can do that, then
we can do IP add

255
00:16:27,730 --> 00:16:34,640
add 10.10.10.10/24 device vethZ.

256
00:16:36,990 --> 00:16:42,445
If I did IP addr inside of it,

257
00:16:42,445 --> 00:16:44,860
it now has an IP address.

258
